---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="Shared-payload TOC pattern"
  description="Multiple TOC entries point to one payload to deduplicate identical lines. 703 of 1,328 message files use this; re-emit must preserve it."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'Message System', href: `${base}/field-guide/message-system/` },
    { label: 'Shared payloads' },
  ]}
>
  <h2>The pattern</h2>
  <p>
    A <a href={`${base}/field-guide/file-formats/reso/`}>RESO</a> TOC entry is
    a <code>(length, data_offset)</code> pair (variant=1, 8 bytes per entry).
    The format allows two TOC entries to carry the same
    <code>data_offset</code> and <code>length</code>, in which case they
    share a single physical payload. The engine reads the payload identically
    regardless of which TOC entry index requested it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Empirical scan across <code>data/message/**/*.ofs</code>:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Files scanned</td><td>1,328</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Files using payload sharing</td><td>703 (52.9%)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Distinct shared groups</td><td>2,890</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Pre-canonicalisation row pairs</td><td>13,422 (6,696 already consistent + 6,726 needing alignment)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Example: msg51016.ofs</h2>
  <p>
    Original file: 3,472 bytes. RESO header variant=1, file_count=100.
    Of the 100 TOC entries, only 27 distinct <code>data_offset</code> values.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Entry range</th>
        <th>Original data_offset</th>
        <th>Payload length</th>
        <th>Shared by N entries</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>0..9</td><td><code>0x0330</code></td><td>158</td><td>10</td></tr>
      <tr><td>10..19</td><td><code>0x03D0</code></td><td>22</td><td>10</td></tr>
      <tr><td>20..29</td><td><code>0x03F0</code></td><td>468</td><td>10</td></tr>
      <tr><td>30..39</td><td><code>0x05D0</code></td><td>34</td><td>10</td></tr>
      <tr><td>40..49</td><td><code>0x0600</code></td><td>34</td><td>10</td></tr>
      <tr><td>60..69</td><td><code>0x0630</code></td><td>472</td><td>10</td></tr>
      <tr><td>73,83</td><td><code>0x08A0</code></td><td>60</td><td>2</td></tr>
      <tr><td>75,85,95</td><td><code>0x08F0</code></td><td>16</td><td>3</td></tr>
      <tr><td>76,86,92</td><td><code>0x0900</code></td><td>144</td><td>3</td></tr>
      <tr><td>78,88</td><td><code>0x0A30</code></td><td>38</td><td>2</td></tr>
      <tr><td>79,89,94</td><td><code>0x0A60</code></td><td>106</td><td>3</td></tr>
      <tr><td>96..99</td><td><code>0x0D70</code></td><td>30</td><td>4</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Unique payload bytes: 2,526. Phantom bytes saved by sharing: 11,412
    (3.5× the payload section if expanded out).
  </p>

  <h2>Why this matters for re-emission</h2>
  <p>
    Without dedup, a fresh payload is written per TOC entry, so a
    100-entry-but-27-payload original becomes a 90+ distinct-payload
    rebuilt file. <code>msg51016.ofs</code> ballooned from
    3,472 B → 19,728 B before the dedup fix. Across the corpus the unfixed
    re-emit added ≈3.54 MB of pure layout waste.
  </p>

  <h2>Dedup logic in re-emit</h2>
  <p>
    From <code>src/translator/reemit.py::rewrite_reso_container</code>
    (variant-1 header, 8-byte entries):
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`header_entry_size = 16 if h.variant == 0 else 8
payload_start     = 0x10 + h.file_count * header_entry_size

new_header        = bytearray()
payload_section   = bytearray()
payload_offsets   : dict[bytes, int] = {}   # payload-bytes -> offset

for ei in range(h.file_count):
    payload = new_payloads.get(ei, b"")
    if len(payload) == 0:
        new_header += struct.pack("<II", 0, 0)
    elif payload in payload_offsets:                     # ← REUSE OFFSET
        offset = payload_offsets[payload]
        new_header += struct.pack("<II", len(payload), offset)
    else:                                                # ← NEW PAYLOAD
        offset = payload_start + len(payload_section)
        payload_offsets[payload] = offset
        payload_section += payload
        pad = (16 - (len(payload) % 16)) % 16            # ← 16-B align
        payload_section += b"\\x00" * pad
        new_header += struct.pack("<II", len(payload), offset)
    if header_entry_size == 16:                          # variant-0: copy type_tag + unk
        new_header += raw[0x10 + ei*16 + 8 : 0x10 + ei*16 + 16]
`}</code></pre>

  <h2>Canonicalisation</h2>
  <p>
    Dedup only helps when the per-entry EN payloads happen to be byte-identical.
    Because each entry was translated independently, two entries that shared a
    JP payload often produced different EN paraphrases. Canonicalisation walks
    each original shared group and forces all members to the same EN string
    before re-emit.
  </p>
  <p>
    Selection rule per group (from <code>shared_payload_canonicalization.md</code>):
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>If any row in the group is marked <code>manual</code>, that row wins.</li>
    <li>Else: longest <code>en_text</code> wins (most likely to fit budget).</li>
    <li>Tie-break: lowest <code>entry_id</code>.</li>
  </ol>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Rows changed by canonicalisation</td><td>6,726</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Manual rows preserved</td><td>3</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Structural mismatches</td><td>0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Bytes recovered from re-emit</td><td>~1.52 MB (43% of bloat)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Top growers (before dedup + canonicalisation)</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>File</th><th>Original</th><th>Patched (naive)</th><th>Dedup savings</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>msg51016</code></td><td>3,472</td><td>19,728</td><td>11,412</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>msg42016</code></td><td>3,184</td><td>19,312</td><td>10,510</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>msg49043</code></td><td>11,040</td><td>27,168</td><td>9,952</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>msg48043</code></td><td>12,368</td><td>28,464</td><td>10,836</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>msg50043</code></td><td>11,184</td><td>26,912</td><td>10,330</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>msg43043</code></td><td>13,008</td><td>28,656</td><td>8,144</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>msg41016</code></td><td>3,136</td><td>18,512</td><td>8,988</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>msg40016</code></td><td>3,200</td><td>18,384</td><td>8,878</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>msg47016</code></td><td>3,200</td><td>18,368</td><td>9,596</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>msg48016</code></td><td>3,344</td><td>18,192</td><td>9,132</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    All ten files are <code>msg{`<N>`}016.ofs</code> or <code>msg{`<N>`}043.ofs</code>
    in the per-chapter career directories. The shared-group pattern is the
    engine's "pick one variant by <code>(npc_id mod N)</code>" table.
  </p>

  <h2>Source</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>src/translator/reemit.py</code> — dedup + emit logic</li>
    <li><code>notes/msg51016_investigation.md</code> — root-cause + numbers</li>
    <li><code>notes/shared_payload_canonicalization.md</code> — canon pass stats</li>
  </ul>
</FieldGuideLayout>
